  "dataCard": {},
  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/corydoras_diphyes_en/",
      "html": "<p>This Corydoras is available irregularly in Germany and was recently described by Axenrot and Kullander (2003).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/corydoras_diphyes_en/",
      "html": "<p>C. diphyes originates from a tributary of the Rio Monday, a river flowing into the Rio Parana in Paraguay. For a long time this species was described as Corydoras flaveolus, which however comes from the Brazilian state of Sao Paulo.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/corydoras_diphyes_en/",
      "html": "<p>This catfish has a variable coloration and is very suitable for the planted community tank together with peaceful companions. In their natural habitat these armoured catfish live in large shoals together with Otocinclus mimulus. The coloration and pattern of O. mimulus coincide very well with those of C. diphyes. In this way the Otocinclus catfish tries to protect itself from predators, in this case Crenicichla lepidota. This phenomenon is called mimicry.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/corydoras_diphyes_en/",
      "html": "<p>This species is relatively easy to breed.</p>"
    }
  ],
